Let $V=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{\infty} V_i$ be a graded vector space over a field $k$, and let $D\colon V\to V$ be a graded linear operator of degree $-1$, that is, $D(V_0)=\{0\}$ and $D(V_{i+1})\subseteq V_i$ for all $i\geq 0$. 

Determine whether it is always possible to equip $V$ with a bilinear product $V\times V\to V$ such that:
(i) the product makes $V$ into a graded $k$-algebra (i.e. $V_i\cdot V_j\subseteq V_{i+j}$);
(ii) the product is associative;
(iii) the algebra has a multiplicative identity $1\in V_0$;
(iv) $D$ is a derivation for this product, that is, $D(ab)=D(a)\,b+a\,D(b)$ for all $a,b\in V$.

Equivalently, characterize those pairs $(V,D)$ as above for which such an associative graded unital algebra structure on $V$ making $D$ a derivation exists.

